# Hoist exactly the three packages named in apps/web/next.config.js's
# `serverExternalPackages`, and nothing else.
#
# Not a preference — measured. Those three are deliberately NOT bundled, so the
# standalone server keeps a bare `require("<name>")` for each. Under npm they
# hoisted to the repo-root `node_modules`, which `next build` copies to the
# standalone ROOT, so the require resolved. Under pnpm's default linker they sit
# only in `apps/web/node_modules` as symlinks, and Next does not carry those
# into the standalone tree: it copies the `.pnpm` store directories plus the
# root-level links. Probed on a copy of `.next/standalone` taken OUT of the repo
# (inside the repo the probe is worthless — resolution escapes upward into the
# source tree and every lookup succeeds):
#
#   UNRESOLVABLE from apps/web/server.js | MODULE_NOT_FOUND   (z3-solver)
#   FAIL pdfkit MODULE_NOT_FOUND
#   ok exceljs  .../node_modules/.pnpm/exceljs@4.4.0/...      (root-level, worked)
#
# exceljs resolving and the other two not is the tell: root-level presence is the
# whole difference. Both failures are silent in production — z3 falls back to LLM
# repair, a designed path, and pdfkit takes the export error path.
#
# This is deliberately three named packages rather than `node-linker=hoisted` or
# `shamefully-hoist`: a blanket hoist restores every one of npm's accidental
# resolutions and with them the class of bug that the strict linker exists to
# surface. Anything added here needs the same kind of evidence.
public-hoist-pattern[]=pdfkit
public-hoist-pattern[]=exceljs
public-hoist-pattern[]=z3-solver
